[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Entity States in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Hibernate and the Java Persistence API (JPA), managing the lifecycle of an entity is fundamental to effective database operations. An entity instance moves through four distinct states during its lifecycle. Understanding these states helps developers prevent common issues like LazyInitializationException and ensures predictable database synchronization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Transient State"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An entity is in the transient state when it has just been instantiated using the Java 'new' operator, but it is not associated with any Hibernate Session (Persistence Context) and has no representation in the database. It is essentially a plain old Java object (POJO)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"User user = new User();
user.setName("John Doe");
user.setEmail("john@example.com");
// At this point, 'user' is Transient."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Persistent State"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"An entity becomes persistent when it is associated with an active Hibernate Session. This can happen by saving a transient entity using methods like persist(), or by retrieving an existing entity from the database using find() or a Criteria/HQL query. Any changes made to a persistent entity are automatically tracked and synchronized with the database during the flush phase."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = new User();
user.setName("Jane Doe");
session.persist(user); // 'user' is now Persistent

tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Detached State"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An entity enters the detached state when the Hibernate Session it was associated with is closed, cleared, or evicted. The entity still holds data corresponding to a database record, but changes made to it will no longer be automatically synchronized with the database. To update a detached entity, it must be re-attached or merged back into a new Session."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
User user = session.find(User.class, 1L);
session.close(); // Session is closed

// 'user' is now Detached. Modifications here will not auto-sync.
user.setName("Updated Name");"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Removed State"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"An entity is in the removed state when it has been scheduled for deletion from the database using the remove() method on the Session. While it is still a Java object in memory during the transaction, Hibernate will execute a SQL DELETE statement when the session is flushed or the transaction is committed."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"Session session = sessionFactory.openSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

User user = session.find(User.class, 1L);
session.remove(user); // 'user' is now Removed

tx.commit(); // Executes SQL DELETE
session.close();"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Entity Lifecycle Transitions"},{"order":16,"bullet_point":"Transient to Persistent: via persist(), save(), or merge()"},{"order":17,"bullet_point":"Persistent to Detached: via close(), clear(), or evict()"},{"order":18,"bullet_point":"Detached to Persistent: via merge() or update()"},{"order":19,"bullet_point":"Persistent to Removed: via remove() or delete()"},{"order":20,"bullet_point":"Removed to Transient: The entity is deleted from the database, but the Java object still exists in memory."}]
